[[!meta title="A First Step"]]
[[!meta author="zaki"]]
[[!meta description=""]]

Parsing Perl (statically) is undecidable. Yes, you heard that correctly.  It is
not unique in this, but it does have some properties which make it trickier
than other programming languages that keep their grammars context-free.
There is a proof of this by Jeffrey Kegler
[1](https://perlmonks.org/?node_id=663393),
[2](https://jeffreykegler.github.io/personal/undecide/undecide.html),
[3](https://blogs.perl.org/users/jeffrey_kegler/2011/10/perl-and-parsing-11-are-all-perl-programs-parseable.html),
because as Kegler writes:

> Perl 5 is unparseable because it gives the programmer Turing-complete power before compile time.

In practice, most Perl **can** be parsed statically (which is why [PPI](https://p3rl.org/PPI) exists),
but this is just the worst-case scenario.

To understand why this is, we have to dig into the specifics of the `perl`
interpreter.

What does it mean by compile time? Well, Perl has
[special code blocks](https://perldoc.perl.org/perlmod#BEGIN%2C-UNITCHECK%2C-CHECK%2C-INIT-and-END)
that it runs at various points as it passes over the code. The big passes can
be considered what are called the *compile phase* and *run phase* and most of the
time, they do exactly what they sound like for each phase, but you can run code
in the compile phase (early processing using [`BEGIN`](https://perldoc.perl.org/functions/BEGIN))
and compile code in the run phase ([`eval`](https://perldoc.perl.org/functions/eval)).

An example is how:

[[!format perl """
print "1\n";
BEGIN { print "2\n" }
print "3\n";
BEGIN { print "4\n" }
"""]]

gives the output

[[!format txt """
2
4
1
3
"""]]

as the `BEGIN` lines fire off first as the source is being read in
(note: `BEGIN`/`END` are from [AWK](https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/html_node/Using-BEGIN_002fEND.html)).

So, what goes on during this compile-phase? Some of this is covered in the
[`perlguts`](https://perldoc.perl.org/perlguts#Compiled-code) documentation: as
it compiles the code, `perl` builds a compile tree (or optree). The individual
nodes of this tree are called `OP`s after the
[`typedef struct op OP;`](https://metacpan.org/release/NWCLARK/perl-5.8.9/source/perl.h#L2222)
with the actual `struct op` and its subclasses (`struct`s that share the same
base) in [op.h](https://metacpan.org/release/NWCLARK/perl-5.8.9/source/op.h#L248).

This optree lives at the C level, but in 1996, Malcolm Beattie worked on "Perl
Compiler Kit"[^compiler-kit-tarball], which Malcolm describes in [an article in The Perl
Journal](https://www.foo.be/docs/tpj/issues/vol1_2/tpj0102-0007.html), that contains the
`B` namespace of modules. This includes the `B.pm` module which constructs Perl
objects that wrap the C side of the optree for walking in Perl code.
It also contains several backends that emit an alternative representation of
the optree:

 - [`B::C`](https://p3rl.org/B::C): freezes the optree as C code that uses the
   Perl internal API to build the optree so that parsing can be skipped;
 - [`B::CC`](https://p3rl.org/B::CC): a partial backend that attempts to be an
   optimising compiler to C (e.g., unrolling operations); and
 - Pretty-printers such as
   [`B::Terse`](https://p3rl.org/B::Terse),
   [`B::Concise`](https://p3rl.org/B::Concise),
   [`B::Deparse`](https://p3rl.org/B::Deparse).

Eventually some of the `B` namespace became part of the Perl distribution
itself since `perl v5.005` (in 1998).

[^compiler-kit-tarball]: The orignal tarballs are on
[BackPAN under author MICB](https://backpan.perl.org/authors/id/M/MI/MICB/) under the names
`Compiler-*.tar.gz`.


